Okay, let me try to figure this out. So Katerina makes necklaces with topaz gemstones and sterling silver beads. The problem is asking how many silver beads she uses per necklace. Let me break down the information given.

First, each necklace has 8 topaz gemstones. Each topaz is 1 inch long. So, the total length taken up by the gemstones themselves would be 8 gemstones multiplied by 1 inch each. That's 8 inches. 

Now, the total length of the necklace is 25 inches. That includes both the gemstones and the silver beads. So the remaining length must be filled with silver beads. Let me subtract the length of the gemstones from the total length. 25 inches minus 8 inches equals 17 inches. So the silver beads take up 17 inches in total.

Each sterling silver bead is one-quarter of an inch long. To find out how many beads make up 17 inches, I need to divide the total length of silver beads by the length of each bead. So that's 17 inches divided by 0.25 inches per bead. 

Wait, dividing by a fraction is the same as multiplying by its reciprocal. So 17 divided by 1/4 is 17 multiplied by 4. That equals 68. So, she uses 68 silver beads per necklace. Let me double-check my steps to make sure.

